  "notes": "Every figure on this row is Ubiquiti's, read 2026-11-21. https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/usw-pro-max-48-poe (HTTP 200, 661,949 B, rendered spec table) prints '1 GbE RJ45 32 (24 PoE+; 8 PoE++) (1G/100M/10M)', '2.5 GbE RJ45 16 (8 PoE+; 8 PoE++) (2.5G/1G/100M/10M)', '10G SFP+ 4 (10G/1G)', 'Max. PoE Output Up to PoE++', 'Total PoE Availability 720W', 'PoE Ports PoE+ 32 / PoE++ 16', 'Max. PoE Wattage per Port by PSE PoE 15.4W / PoE+ 32W / PoE++ 64W'. There is no datasheet text layer to cross-check: https://dl.ui.com/ds/usw-pro-max-48-poe_ds.pdf answers 200 with the 609,911-byte techspecs front page, not a PDF. The 32 PoE+ and 16 PoE++ counts land exactly on the two speed banks, so the PoE++ sockets are modelled as the upper 8 of each bank - Ports 25-32 on the 1G bank and Ports 41-48 on the 2.5G bank - the ordering this catalogue already applies to USW-Pro-Max-24-PoE; undeclared, all 48 sockets reported the 15.4 W 802.3af default instead. Before this correction no copper port on the row declared poeOut, so engine/media.js's undeclared-port default gave every socket the 802.3af ceiling of 15.4 W and web/faceplate.js printed 'PoE out 15.4 W' on sockets the datasheet rates 802.3at/802.3bt. The SFP+ cages are not PSE ports and stay undeclared."
